  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Small hydropower center of excellence in Nigeria</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Nigeria, endowed with vast natural resources and a rapidly growing population, faces significant challenges in ensuring reliable and sustainable energy access. Currently, 92 million Nigerians lack electricity, exacerbating socio-economic disparities and hindering industrial growth. The Nigerian Government#s 2021 Energy Transition Plan (ETP) seeks to address energy poverty and climate change by achieving universal electricity access by 2030 and net-zero emissions by 2060. This ambitious plan underscores the urgent need for innovative and scalable renewable energy solutions.  Small hydropower (SHP) is a proven, efficient renewable energy technology particularly well-suited for rural electrification. Despite Africa&apos;s substantial SHP potential of 15,714 MW, less than 5% has been developed due to technical, financial, and policy barriers. Recognizing the transformative role of SHP in overcoming these challenges, the United Nations Industrial Development Organization (UNIDO) proposes the establishment of a Small Hydropower Center of Excellence in Nigeria under the China-Africa-UN Collaboration Framework.  The Center will serve as a hub for SHP knowledge transfer, technical training, and policy guidance, benefiting both Nigeria and the broader West African region. Aligned with the Global Development Initiative on Clim</narrative>
  </description>
